[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that collapses the scope substantially, and I want to say so plainly: [1mthis is not a notifications system, it's
[0m  [1mdue-date reminders in a single-user page.[0m Much smaller, and the parts I was worried about (identity, subscriptions,
  fan-out, delivery infrastructure) all fall away. "Tasks they care about" becomes just "tasks with a due date."

  Now the constraint that will decide the design, and it's the one most likely to disappoint you later:

  [1mA browser-local app can only remind you while the page is open.[0m If the tab is closed, nothing runs — there's no server
  and no process. Three ways out, and none is free:

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a banner/badge for tasks due soon, computed on load and on a timer while open. Honest about what it
    is. Costs nothing, no permission prompt. But if you close the tab and the deadline passes, you learn about it next
    time you visit.
  - [1mOS notifications (Notification API)[0m — a real system toast. Needs a permission prompt, and still [1monly fires while the
[0m    [1mpage is open[0m unless you add a service worker. Modest cost, better presence.
  - [1mService worker + installed PWA[0m — the only browser-local path that can fire with the tab closed, and even then
    background-sync scheduling is Chromium-only and unreliable. Significant complexity for a guarantee you don't
    actually get.

  If "remind me when I'm not looking" is the actual requirement, none of these deliver it properly and a small server
  would — worth knowing before we commit.

  My recommendation: [1min-page only[0m, with OS notifications as a thin layer on top later if you want them. It's the honest
  version, it's cheap, and it doesn't pretend to a reliability the platform can't provide.
